From: Kyvenza <support@kyvenza.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:03:55 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-gpu-virgl: do not use map_fixed on macOS

Hypervisor.framework appears to keep mapping the pages that backed the
hostmem region when it was registered: replacing them in place with MAP_FIXED
is not seen by the guest and blob contents stay stale. On Windows/Triton the
second WebGL page in a boot renders nothing. Always use the subregion
method there.
---
 hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
index 9b763ea83c8..17f8070c8c4 100644
--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -193,13 +193,22 @@ virtio_gpu_virgl_map_resource_blob(VirtIOGPU *g,
      * granule on a 16 KiB-page host (hvf ipa-granule-size=0x1000 on Apple
      * silicon) the guest may place a blob at any 4 KiB offset; use the
      * subregion method for those.
+     *
+     * On macOS, Hypervisor.framework keeps mapping the pages that backed
+     * hostmem when it was registered; replacing them in place with MAP_FIXED
+     * is not seen by the guest (the blob reads stay stale), so never use
+     * MAP_FIXED there.
      */
+#ifdef CONFIG_DARWIN
+    ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
+#else
     if (!QEMU_IS_ALIGNED(offset, qemu_real_host_page_size())) {
         ret = -EOPNOTSUPP;
     } else {
         ret = virgl_renderer_resource_map_fixed(res->base.resource_id,
                                                 gl->hostmem_mmap + offset);
     }
+#endif
     switch (ret) {
     case 0:
         res->map_fixed = gl->hostmem_mmap + offset;
